WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.500
So my name is Angus Main. I'm the interim head of program for Information

2
00:00:04.561 --> 00:00:05.280
Experience Design.

3
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.930
I'm going to be taking you through a bit of an introduction to IED today.

4
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.280
So talking through a bit of the structure of the course, what we teach, how we

5
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.410
teach it. And then beyond that is a chance for you guys to ask questions.

6
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.270
So if you've got any questions as we go through, going to keep it simple by just

7
00:00:24.631 --> 00:00:30.420
using the chat. So if you have any kind of questions as I'm talking or at the end

8
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:30.991
of the session,

9
00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:36.390
feel free to use the webinar chat, add your question in there, and I'll go through

10
00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.161
those as we get them in.

11
00:00:40.411 --> 00:00:43.231
Okay, I'm going to make a start by sharing a presentation.

12
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.540
So bear with me as I just set that up.

13
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.690
So welcome to Information Experience Design.

14
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.350
I'm going to take you through a little bit about the program, and I'm going to

15
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.551
really start by thinking about

16
00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.620
what is information experience design. This is maybe a new area for you.

17
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.930
Hopefully, you've read something about the program already.

18
00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:05.280
You've learned something. Maybe you've seen some of the student work, seen some of

19
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.040
the exhibitions we do, seen some of our fantastic alumni.

20
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.301
But information experience design itself is maybe an intriguing title.

21
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.480
It's a title that not necessarily everyone knows straight away.

22
00:01:19.051 --> 00:01:22.440
So I want to unpack that a little bit and sort of talk about what it is and what we

23
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.211
do on IED.

24
00:01:25.801 --> 00:01:31.680
So what is IED? The simple question is we don't actually

25
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.051
know completely at this stage in the year.

26
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.521
So here we are at the beginning of the term for 2026.

27
00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.230
We've got a new intake of students.

28
00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:45.780
We've got a fantastic group of students all coming in with interesting new ideas,

29
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:50.101
interesting questions, interesting concepts, and we haven't found out what those

30
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.280
are yet. So they're starting to work in groups.

31
00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.070
They're starting to go through our teaching experience.

32
00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.851
And what will emerge over the year is a set of practices, a set of questions, a set

33
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.700
of interests, perhaps, which are unique to that group, which are those students'

34
00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:13.351
response to these concepts of information, experience, and design.

35
00:02:13.921 --> 00:02:17.520
So I can show you what IED was last year, and I can show that by showing you the

36
00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.960
student work, telling you about what they were interested in, what kind of methods

37
00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.990
they were exploring, and we will do that at the end of this presentation.

38
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.360
But for when you come in, I want you to understand that actually there's a lot of

39
00:02:30.391 --> 00:02:32.040
flexibility in what you're able to do.

40
00:02:32.101 --> 00:02:35.370
You can come in and you can bring your interest, and you can really shape the

41
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.351
program. You can shape what we mean by IED, and you working alongside your

42
00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:45.090
colleagues and the other IED students and working alongside the staff will really

43
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.040
push forward what we mean by IED.

44
00:02:48.391 --> 00:02:52.260
So yeah. So that's the kind of idea, that there's flexibility, that actually we are

45
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.970
in between lots of different areas.

46
00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:59.010
So we're a design course, we have design in the title, but actually we do lots of

47
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.440
different things, and we have people from various types of practice.

48
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.800
So we also borrow heavily from fine art practice.

49
00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.630
We borrow from other kind of creative practices. We borrow from the sciences.

50
00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:14.309
We sit in this hyper-position between lots of different types of work and lots of

51
00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.060
different types of practice. And I think that's really exciting, and it gives you a

52
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.500
lot of opportunity to be exploring different kind of work with your time with IED.

53
00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.110
So that's kind of the overall, but obviously there is a bit more structure to it

54
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.990
than that, and there is some core things that we are looking for.

55
00:03:31.201 --> 00:03:33.390
So the most clearest definition

56
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.890
that goes across all of IED work, no matter what students are bringing in, what

57
00:03:37.921 --> 00:03:41.671
other kinds of practice they're looking at, is this.

58
00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:47.640
We transform information into experiences. So we are rooted in communication.

59
00:03:48.031 --> 00:03:51.720
We're rooted in the School of Communication and Design, and we are one of the

60
00:03:51.781 --> 00:03:54.480
programs that has emerged from the communication side of that.

61
00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.730
So a lot of what we do, no matter what kind of tools you might be using or what

62
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.720
kind of work you want to do, we will be looking through the lens of communication.

63
00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.830
So it starts with information. We're thinking about experiences that communicate

64
00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.440
things, and those experiences can be all kinds of stuff.

65
00:04:10.531 --> 00:04:14.101
It might be a VR experience, it might be an installation, it might be something

66
00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.351
site-specific, it might be performance.

67
00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.570
But any one of those things you could do, but they should all be thinking about

68
00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.050
communication. Should be thinking about what are you trying to say through this?

69
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.820
If you're creating a performance, if you're creating an amazing multimodal,

70
00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.500
digital, physical experience for people, then what are you trying to say?

71
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.430
What are you trying to get across? What's the information that you are creating

72
00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.091
within this experience, and how will that be communicated to audiences?

73
00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:48.210
So taking information, which might be stories, it might be questions, it might be

74
00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:49.890
facts you're trying to get across.

75
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.070
It might be almost more like journalism that you're trying to say, or it might be

76
00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.800
data. It might be big data, small data.

77
00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.050
Whatever that information is, you will be choosing appropriate experiences to tell

78
00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.940
that information, to tell that story.

79
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.620
So that transformation is the main thing that we're focused on.

80
00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.150
So taking information, transforming it into experiences.

81
00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.281
You can see that arrow going across the top here and thinking about it through

82
00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:21.180
methods of design, and I use that term design broadly.

83
00:05:21.781 --> 00:05:25.531
So they might be physical types of design. You might be making things.

84
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.130
They might be design methodologies around certain technologies that you're

85
00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:29.880
interested in.

86
00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.800
But also you might be, as I say, borrowing from artistic practice.

87
00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.550
You might be producing paintings. You might be producing sculpture.

88
00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.010
You might be doing all kinds of things actually, which fits maybe a bit more

89
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.040
comfortably into sort of artistic space. Or you might be writing.

90
00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.000
It might be that actually this is a written work, or a performance or whatever.

91
00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.890
So whatever the correct method is for that particular outcome that you're

92
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.890
interested in, that's what you'll be engaging with, thinking, how do I take this

93
00:05:55.951 --> 00:05:59.220
information, this story I'm trying to tell, and turn it into an experience?

94
00:05:59.281 --> 00:06:00.570
And what can that experience be?

95
00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.940
Really think about the sort of the multimodal aspects, the multisensory, thinking

96
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.120
about experiences that work across all kinds of different outcomes, across

97
00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.170
different senses, across time. You'll be exploring all that, and we'll be getting

98
00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:16.830
richly into kind of experience and what we mean by experience.

99
00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:21.180
But I also want to highlight the bottom arrow here, that actually that works in the

100
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:25.860
other direction as well. So we can, and our students do, move from experience into

101
00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:29.160
information. And actually, this is the first step that we're going to be asking you

102
00:06:29.189 --> 00:06:33.270
to be thinking about. Because the information that you're going to be placing into

103
00:06:33.331 --> 00:06:36.300
these experiences, the question will be, well, where's that information coming

104
00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.600
from? What stories are you trying to tell? Whose stories are you trying to tell?

105
00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.270
What data is it? Where did that data come from?

106
00:06:42.331 --> 00:06:44.640
And what are you then trying to do with that data?

107
00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:49.350
So the source of the information, the source of the data or the stories or the

108
00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.790
messages that you're trying to work with, create experiences about, that's really

109
00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:58.860
important. And that process really is about taking experiences, so experience of

110
00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:02.460
the real world, maybe a particular individual's experience or someone's story, or

111
00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.170
the story of a community or a collection of people, finding those stories, finding

112
00:07:07.201 --> 00:07:10.680
that data. It might be environmental data about a certain place.

113
00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.880
It might be climate data from the oceans, all kinds of data.

114
00:07:15.451 --> 00:07:18.210
But we want you to engage with that process of finding that data.

115
00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:22.860
In other words, research. So taking experiences out there in the real world,

116
00:07:23.311 --> 00:07:24.780
transforming that into information.

117
00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.401
So doing the research, finding these stories, finding the data, which you will then

118
00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.331
turn into experiences for other people, translating it into something that people

119
00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:38.341
can see, hear, understand. So that's our general methodology.

120
00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:42.360
But there's lots of flexibility within the type of design you're doing or the type

121
00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.990
of research you're doing. So the type of design we might think of as methods of

122
00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:49.890
production. So these are the things you're actually going to be doing to make your

123
00:07:49.951 --> 00:07:52.350
project, and that will depend on what the project is.

124
00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:53.670
And you may already have some ideas.

125
00:07:53.701 --> 00:07:56.670
You may be working in a certain area that you know, I really want to work with

126
00:07:56.730 --> 00:08:00.750
sound, so I want to work with animation or performance. And that's fine.

127
00:08:00.811 --> 00:08:04.320
You can bring those in. But also, those are things you might explore and discover

128
00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:08.490
through your time on the program. So you might actually attend some workshops and

129
00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.140
find all about AI or physical computing, or find some new techniques of filmmaking

130
00:08:13.651 --> 00:08:16.710
that actually you hadn't encountered before, and they become part of your practice,

131
00:08:16.831 --> 00:08:19.740
part of the production that you're doing to create your experiences.

132
00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:22.740
So there are lots of different methods of production.

133
00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.280
There's lots of skills that students bring into IED.

134
00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:30.390
We encourage those to be shared as well. So a big part of IED is collaboration.

135
00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:34.591
So you may not be a filmmaker, but the person sitting next to you might be an

136
00:08:34.651 --> 00:08:38.100
accomplished filmmaker already, and actually, we can swap skills, you can

137
00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:41.461
collaborate. You'll be encouraged to collaborate as much as possible so that we can

138
00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:45.300
create experiences that go across lots of different modes of practice, lots of

139
00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.590
different types of discipline.

140
00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:51.660
And these are some of the kinds of production you might be using on IED.

141
00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:55.830
All of these are supported. We have a workshop, we have technical staff who can

142
00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:58.980
help with this, as well as our tutor staff who have experience in these different

143
00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:04.080
areas. So you can learn more about these. But this isn't a finite list.

144
00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:07.470
You can be adding to this, and we do add to it every year when we find interesting

145
00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:12.570
new areas, and there's all kinds of niche types of production or methods of making

146
00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:16.681
that people will discover or bring into their practice through their time on IED.

147
00:09:18.295 --> 00:09:21.205
And then for the other direction as well, methods of research equally is really

148
00:09:21.264 --> 00:09:24.504
broad. We will be getting you to think about where you find your data.

149
00:09:24.534 --> 00:09:27.984
We'll be getting you to think about ethnography, in other words, working with

150
00:09:28.045 --> 00:09:30.474
people, talking to people, finding their stories.

151
00:09:30.864 --> 00:09:34.494
But you'll also be working with other forms, maybe more quantitative forms of

152
00:09:34.734 --> 00:09:37.974
research, finding out the data that you're interested in.

153
00:09:38.514 --> 00:09:41.304
You'll also be researching the kind of production you want to do.

154
00:09:41.455 --> 00:09:46.884
So looking at the materials, researching materials, methods, tools, experimenting,

155
00:09:47.004 --> 00:09:52.284
trying things out, taking a tool or a material that maybe has a certain use, but

156
00:09:52.344 --> 00:09:55.344
experimenting with that, finding different usage, finding different ways to use it

157
00:09:55.404 --> 00:09:58.284
that might be more interesting, more suited for your particular experience that

158
00:09:58.314 --> 00:10:02.274
you're dealing with. So there's a very experimental approach we take to both

159
00:10:02.304 --> 00:10:04.434
research and to production as well.

160
00:10:06.624 --> 00:10:10.314
So in terms of the IED philosophy, hopefully I've captured some of that already,

161
00:10:10.854 --> 00:10:11.034
but

162
00:10:12.264 --> 00:10:15.564
we are interdisciplinary, so we work across disciplines.

163
00:10:15.624 --> 00:10:17.575
We don't just take people from one type of discipline.

164
00:10:17.634 --> 00:10:21.504
We don't just have students graduating with one type of skills or one type of

165
00:10:21.564 --> 00:10:26.964
discipline work. Along with lots of other courses in the RCA, we try and bring this

166
00:10:26.994 --> 00:10:31.584
together. We try and really think across disciplines, between disciplines, and

167
00:10:31.614 --> 00:10:34.164
allowing you as an individual practitioner to do that.

168
00:10:34.224 --> 00:10:38.214
So learning how to collaborate well, learning how to work ethically with others,

169
00:10:38.334 --> 00:10:40.314
bring other ideas into your production.

170
00:10:42.234 --> 00:10:44.154
We're thinking about transformative communication.

171
00:10:44.634 --> 00:10:48.504
So you're creating some kind of experience, which is communicative.

172
00:10:48.684 --> 00:10:52.764
So it's an experience that people will have, your audience will have, that it

173
00:10:52.795 --> 00:10:55.494
should speak to in some way. It should have a message, it should communicate

174
00:10:55.554 --> 00:10:59.604
something. And that could be all kinds of things, but it should be some form of

175
00:10:59.664 --> 00:11:03.834
communication. But we want communication that transforms, that has an impact.

176
00:11:04.374 --> 00:11:07.344
So for the people you're trying to speak to with these experiences, the people who

177
00:11:07.404 --> 00:11:10.644
encounter the information experiences that you're designing,

178
00:11:11.904 --> 00:11:14.455
you should really think about that audience and the impact you want to have on

179
00:11:14.484 --> 00:11:16.344
them. What is the message you want to get across to them?

180
00:11:16.404 --> 00:11:18.384
What is the behavior you want to change?

181
00:11:18.474 --> 00:11:22.795
What is the set of facts or information that is really going to make a difference

182
00:11:22.884 --> 00:11:24.714
if you can get it out there into the world?

183
00:11:25.194 --> 00:11:28.344
And then think about the experiences that you're going to use to do that.

184
00:11:29.754 --> 00:11:31.734
We think about experiential research.

185
00:11:31.884 --> 00:11:36.054
So in other words, going out there, trying things, testing things, and not just

186
00:11:36.114 --> 00:11:39.564
relying on Google searches, not just looking at a data set, but actually going out

187
00:11:39.594 --> 00:11:43.705
there to experience the kind of information that you're going to bring into your

188
00:11:43.734 --> 00:11:46.734
projects. We think about co-creative methods.

189
00:11:46.974 --> 00:11:50.784
Again, collaboration is really key with this sort of cross-disciplinary work that

190
00:11:50.814 --> 00:11:55.884
we do. You can't necessarily be an expert on every type of production, but you can

191
00:11:55.974 --> 00:11:59.424
work with people who are, and you can bring that expertise in or bring in these

192
00:11:59.484 --> 00:12:03.024
skills into your project. And equally, you can share your skills and your knowledge

193
00:12:03.054 --> 00:12:05.724
with other people. There'll be lots of that within the program.

194
00:12:06.624 --> 00:12:08.274
We think about speculative design a lot.

195
00:12:08.304 --> 00:12:11.634
So we think about futures and how these experiences that we're creating might

196
00:12:11.694 --> 00:12:14.424
affect futures or might represent certain futures.

197
00:12:14.844 --> 00:12:18.414
And working with critical design and world-building and these kind of ideas that go

198
00:12:18.474 --> 00:12:19.045
with that.

199
00:12:20.274 --> 00:12:21.984
And I would say we're critically optimistic.

200
00:12:22.344 --> 00:12:25.584
In other words, you'll be asked to be critical about what you're doing.

201
00:12:25.644 --> 00:12:29.004
If you're using a particular method or if you're trying to use a certain piece of

202
00:12:29.034 --> 00:12:32.604
information or you're telling a certain story, we want you to look at that

203
00:12:32.633 --> 00:12:35.754
critically and question it. Is this the best use of this technology?

204
00:12:35.814 --> 00:12:37.404
Is this the right thing to be saying?

205
00:12:37.494 --> 00:12:40.344
Is this going to have the impact that you think it is in the world?

206
00:12:40.914 --> 00:12:44.124
So being critical about that. But we also want to be optimistic.

207
00:12:44.575 --> 00:12:47.545
We want these better futures that we're working towards.

208
00:12:47.634 --> 00:12:50.664
We want these experiences which hopefully are going to change things in a positive

209
00:12:50.724 --> 00:12:54.894
way. And we're optimistic about the methods that we're using, the technologies

210
00:12:54.954 --> 00:12:58.704
we're using, that they could be good, that they could lead to better futures.

211
00:12:59.604 --> 00:13:01.585
But we balance that with the criticality as well.

212
00:13:03.684 --> 00:13:08.184
In terms of the structure, so the kind of things that we teach and how we teach

213
00:13:08.244 --> 00:13:11.784
them. First thing I should say is that what I'm going to take you through is the

214
00:13:11.814 --> 00:13:15.144
structure for this year, and it's the units that we're teaching this year.

215
00:13:16.164 --> 00:13:19.284
As with all programs, we go through regular reviews, and we go through regular

216
00:13:19.314 --> 00:13:21.085
revalidations. We've got one of those coming up.

217
00:13:21.445 --> 00:13:23.484
So some of these titles may change slightly.

218
00:13:23.545 --> 00:13:27.265
The order that they appear in the year and things may change. They may tweak.

219
00:13:27.325 --> 00:13:28.704
That's all subject to validation.

220
00:13:29.664 --> 00:13:33.265
But I'll take you through the basic core elements now because those are likely to

221
00:13:33.325 --> 00:13:34.945
stay, and they will stay in some form.

222
00:13:35.154 --> 00:13:36.984
They might just be framed slightly differently.

223
00:13:38.124 --> 00:13:42.414
And obviously when we get nearer to you hopefully starting with us, we will have

224
00:13:42.445 --> 00:13:45.744
the updated list. You'll be able to see all the kind of latest version of the

225
00:13:45.774 --> 00:13:47.515
program specification and how we teach it.

226
00:13:48.174 --> 00:13:53.515
But for this year, how we've been doing up to now, we think about the first term.

227
00:13:53.604 --> 00:13:56.844
We have a unit called Form and Methods. This is an IED unit.

228
00:13:57.325 --> 00:14:01.404
It's about developing a deep understanding of the methods, skills, and approaches

229
00:14:01.554 --> 00:14:02.874
which are relevant to your practice.

230
00:14:03.564 --> 00:14:08.664
And it's about practical experimentation and testing and developing the context for

231
00:14:08.724 --> 00:14:13.224
your work. So form and methods. So it's the methods really that you're thinking

232
00:14:13.284 --> 00:14:17.904
about and we'll get a lot of work into. And through that, then developing the form.

233
00:14:18.024 --> 00:14:20.575
So what form of experience are you trying to create?

234
00:14:21.144 --> 00:14:23.664
And what methods are you going to use to get to that point?

235
00:14:24.474 --> 00:14:27.924
And again, remember, those methods are methods of production, so making this

236
00:14:27.984 --> 00:14:31.644
experience. But they're also methods of research, which is really crucial in time

237
00:14:31.734 --> 00:14:35.424
one. How are you finding out this information? How are you finding your audience?

238
00:14:35.515 --> 00:14:40.134
How are you testing things? What are the stories you want to tell, and how are you

239
00:14:40.195 --> 00:14:43.314
finding those out? So that will happen in time one.

240
00:14:44.604 --> 00:14:49.554
Time two, we move over to situated practice, which is the next IED unit, and that's

241
00:14:49.585 --> 00:14:52.254
mixed with this unit called core skills. They really come together.

242
00:14:52.284 --> 00:14:54.594
Again, this is how it's arranged for this year.

243
00:14:55.674 --> 00:14:57.295
So this is about refining your methods.

244
00:14:57.864 --> 00:15:02.335
So thinking about those methods you identified in term one, really testing this,

245
00:15:02.364 --> 00:15:07.015
really becoming experts in those methods through term two, trying to adapt them,

246
00:15:07.104 --> 00:15:10.494
trying to evolve them into something which is going to create this amazing

247
00:15:10.524 --> 00:15:12.024
experience at the end of that process.

248
00:15:13.075 --> 00:15:16.524
But it's also working out the situation, how your work is situated in the world.

249
00:15:16.554 --> 00:15:20.064
What's your context? You had a bit of that in term one as well, but term two,

250
00:15:20.124 --> 00:15:21.924
again, you're going to develop that further.

251
00:15:22.374 --> 00:15:25.554
So you're going to be testing your work in situ. Where does this work exist?

252
00:15:25.734 --> 00:15:29.124
Who is it for? Finding that audience, trying it in that place.

253
00:15:29.124 --> 00:15:33.234
So you're not just working just in the studio in the RCA, you're working out there

254
00:15:33.234 --> 00:15:35.424
in the real world, you're testing things in the real world.

255
00:15:36.744 --> 00:15:39.774
And this all leads towards your independent research project in term three.

256
00:15:40.284 --> 00:15:43.253
So this is when actually you're really going to deliver this project.

257
00:15:43.585 --> 00:15:47.273
So throughout terms one, two, and three, you're working towards

258
00:15:48.804 --> 00:15:53.484
one sort of project, one main overall project, and you'll develop this.

259
00:15:54.024 --> 00:15:57.534
So it's helpful if you're coming in maybe with knowing, look, there are certain

260
00:15:57.594 --> 00:16:01.015
questions I'm interested in asking, or there's a certain type of work I really want

261
00:16:01.075 --> 00:16:06.954
to do as part of this MA. And we will help you evolve that, develop it, find

262
00:16:07.015 --> 00:16:09.534
the right direction for that through terms one and two.

263
00:16:09.594 --> 00:16:10.884
In term three, you'll be delivering that.

264
00:16:11.304 --> 00:16:14.335
So delivering your final work in the context in which you want to practice.

265
00:16:14.634 --> 00:16:19.075
So again, out there in the real world, working independently to stage test and

266
00:16:19.134 --> 00:16:21.924
document your work and understand its impact on audiences.

267
00:16:22.314 --> 00:16:24.835
That's really important, testing it with audiences.

268
00:16:25.554 --> 00:16:27.144
You could create amazing experiences.

269
00:16:27.174 --> 00:16:32.064
You could have a fantastic augmented reality experience with multi-sensory inputs

270
00:16:32.094 --> 00:16:34.825
and outputs, and that could be great.

271
00:16:35.335 --> 00:16:39.174
What we really want to see from an assessment point of view is, has that worked?

272
00:16:39.204 --> 00:16:41.604
Has that communicated what you want it to communicate?

273
00:16:41.814 --> 00:16:45.684
In other words, does your audience take from that what you intended them to take

274
00:16:45.744 --> 00:16:49.104
from it? And you can only really know that if you test it with audiences.

275
00:16:49.464 --> 00:16:51.774
So there's testing that will happen as part of that process as well.

276
00:16:52.134 --> 00:16:55.974
It's a really useful phase that allows you really to adapt and improve and find new

277
00:16:56.034 --> 00:17:00.564
ways into your work that maybe weren't visible before you did that testing.

278
00:17:01.644 --> 00:17:03.144
So that happens in term three.

279
00:17:04.434 --> 00:17:06.265
There's other elements which, again, for this year, is

280
00:17:07.975 --> 00:17:12.354
what we teach across the year, and actually sits beyond the IED teaching, and these

281
00:17:12.384 --> 00:17:14.634
will continue in some form for next year as well.

282
00:17:15.234 --> 00:17:19.644
So there are various points where you can work with others outside of the program.

283
00:17:19.854 --> 00:17:21.535
So everything I've just talked about is IED.

284
00:17:21.654 --> 00:17:25.884
You'll be working with other IED students, hopefully collaborating outside of that

285
00:17:25.914 --> 00:17:28.614
as well, but the teaching all happens within IED.

286
00:17:29.574 --> 00:17:32.784
And these two that we're looking at here, across RCA, you're making lots of others.

287
00:17:33.204 --> 00:17:36.594
These are cross-school and cross-college units.

288
00:17:36.624 --> 00:17:41.784
So they allow you to collaborate with people, not just on IED, but from other

289
00:17:41.844 --> 00:17:46.194
programs within the School of Communication and Design, and also just broadly

290
00:17:46.254 --> 00:17:49.434
across the whole of the college. And also working beyond the college to think about

291
00:17:49.464 --> 00:17:51.654
the communities around us and working with them.

292
00:17:52.074 --> 00:17:55.044
So there are various points you will do that, and that can be really helpful for

293
00:17:55.134 --> 00:17:58.824
expanding your network, finding other people to work with, finding people with very

294
00:17:58.884 --> 00:18:01.554
different disciplines, perhaps from your own or even the ones that are represented

295
00:18:01.584 --> 00:18:06.654
within IED. So making collaborators, which really will then take you into your

296
00:18:06.714 --> 00:18:09.894
final project and improve your work from that.

297
00:18:11.604 --> 00:18:13.584
So the types of teaching we have on IED,

298
00:18:14.844 --> 00:18:19.374
it breaks down in various ways. So one of the core things we have, again, for the

299
00:18:19.434 --> 00:18:23.394
current year and should continue in some form for the next year, is field

300
00:18:23.424 --> 00:18:28.194
collectives. So field collectives are smaller groups of students.

301
00:18:28.704 --> 00:18:33.054
So it's usually around 14 students in a group, and it's led by one tutor.

302
00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:36.480
And the idea is these are slightly curated.

303
00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:40.200
You're doing it slightly by students selecting preferences for the kind of things

304
00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:43.230
they're interested in, and then we'll put you in a group with a tutor.

305
00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:47.850
And within that group, you will have people with a mixture of interests which align

306
00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:50.580
with your own. They might not all be exactly the same interest, and you might not

307
00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:53.010
have the same type of background. In fact, we try not to do that.

308
00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:57.000
We try and make an interesting mix of people, but where we can see connections

309
00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:58.500
between the kind of things you're trying to do.

310
00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:01.740
Maybe there's a certain set of questions that you're all interested in.

311
00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:05.400
Maybe you're all interested in a certain approach or a certain type of theory that

312
00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:06.600
you might be applying to your work.

313
00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:10.470
And a tutor will lead that, and it'll be based very much on their own practice as

314
00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:13.500
well. So you'll get their expertise and their research and practice.

315
00:19:14.371 --> 00:19:18.540
And they will set some briefs, they will set some provocations, they will set some

316
00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:20.460
readings and things to learn about.

317
00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:23.940
They will take you through maybe some new methods or some new types of theory.

318
00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:28.080
And then as a collective, you'll work together, and you'll also work individually

319
00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:30.871
to progress your projects through term one and term two.

320
00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:36.030
When we get to term three, then you're more independently working, so we're beyond

321
00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:37.170
the field collectives at that stage.

322
00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:40.080
But for terms one and two, that's the main point of teaching.

323
00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:45.180
We also have personal tutorials. So as well as a tutor who is leading your field

324
00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:49.530
collective group, so a sort of tutor group, we'll also have a personal tutor who

325
00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:52.621
you meet with individually and can just guide you through the whole year.

326
00:19:52.801 --> 00:19:55.650
So you'll be with them the whole year. You'll be meeting with them regularly.

327
00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:57.690
You can talk to them about your work.

328
00:19:57.720 --> 00:20:01.140
They can give advice and feedback as a sort of slightly third party on your work.

329
00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:04.111
They can take a step back and say, "Okay, this is really interesting.

330
00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:06.361
This looks like where it's heading." They know you.

331
00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:11.429
They know what you're trying to aim to and get to through the MA, so they can guide

332
00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:12.720
you through that process as well.

333
00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:17.551
And they can also help with pastoral care and things which are maybe nothing to do

334
00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:21.390
with the teaching, but just questions you have, issues you might encounter.

335
00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:23.310
They can support you through that as well.

336
00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:28.260
And then outside of sort of the individual and smaller group work, we have lectures

337
00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.150
and workshops. So we meet regularly.

338
00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:34.560
So every week, at the moment, we're meeting on Thursdays, and the whole cohort

339
00:20:34.621 --> 00:20:38.730
comes together, and we go through some lectures and some sort of big group

340
00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:40.920
workshops. So we take you through ideas, basically.

341
00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:42.930
So it's our core concepts, we call it.

342
00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.830
And so these big ideas that relate to experience. What is experience?

343
00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:50.010
What is information? What is design? What methods are we using?

344
00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:54.900
So lots of ideas are packed into those sessions. And that's with the whole group.

345
00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:58.650
We come together. It's a bit more of a lecture format, but with seminars and

346
00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:01.051
breakouts and things, so you can discuss things as well.

347
00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:03.121
And that's everyone coming together.

348
00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:07.890
We also have professional practice talks and workshops, so we have people coming

349
00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:12.210
into the college from outside of the teaching team, maybe to talk through their own

350
00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:14.790
practice. So that might be practicing artists or designers.

351
00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:19.290
It might be people from particular agencies that have an affiliation with IED.

352
00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:22.710
Quite often as well, it's alumni and people who have already been through this

353
00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:26.130
process of IED and are now doing fantastic work out there in the real world, and

354
00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:29.460
they come back and talk about their work and offer advice as well.

355
00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:32.551
And throughout all of these, we have crits as well.

356
00:21:32.611 --> 00:21:34.470
So that's a big part of the teaching as well.

357
00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:37.560
So particularly in the field collectives, you're going to be talking to each other.

358
00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:40.440
So it's not just the tutors, the teachers talking to you.

359
00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:45.060
There's also the opportunity, and we make as many opportunities as possible to give

360
00:21:45.121 --> 00:21:50.130
feedback to each other, to present your work, show it to people, and get people's

361
00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:53.730
ideas, get people's suggestions, and that sort of compassionate critique.

362
00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:57.960
So not being critical necessarily about work, but really thinking, "Okay, that's

363
00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:01.260
interesting if you thought about this as well." And that comes as much from other

364
00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:04.590
students and the great wealth of knowledge we have within our students as much as

365
00:22:04.621 --> 00:22:06.570
it comes from the tutors during those crits.

366
00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:12.420
Other types of RCA teaching. So as well as the kind of stuff we provide and the

367
00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:15.780
teaching we do, just being part of the RCA, you're going to access a lot of

368
00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:18.450
technical services, technical teaching, so you're going to be able to access

369
00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:24.060
workshops, inductions into certain tools or processes from all over the college.

370
00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:26.611
Lots of different specialist workshops that you're going to be able to get

371
00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:31.230
inductions on or go and visit or try and do a particular piece of work or speak to

372
00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:33.900
a particular technician who has expertise in that area.

373
00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:39.990
We have academic skills in EAP, so supporting language skills, but also just

374
00:22:40.111 --> 00:22:43.710
academic skills. So you're going to be doing some writing as part of the program.

375
00:22:43.950 --> 00:22:46.710
Not a huge amount, but we are asking you to think about your ideas in an

376
00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:48.030
appropriate sort of academic way.

377
00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:53.460
So that kind of writing, that reviewing work, research methods, there's a team to

378
00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.880
support that. And then there's also lots of events. Lots of them.

379
00:22:56.940 --> 00:23:02.520
So we have events within the program at the end of each term, leading up then to a

380
00:23:02.551 --> 00:23:05.611
sort of final festival showcase at the end of the year.

381
00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:10.290
But because what we're producing is experiences, and we want you to show those

382
00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:14.130
experiences to as many people as possible, we really encourage you to stage them in

383
00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:16.230
various ways throughout the year.

384
00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:19.950
So each field collective, quite often field collectives organize their own little

385
00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:24.720
mini shows. Maybe particular interest groups get together and say, "Let's put

386
00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:28.470
together a show about this or an event or a talk." So there's lots of things

387
00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:31.740
happening. There's lots of things that happen across the whole college around that.

388
00:23:33.390 --> 00:23:37.020
Finally, I just want to talk about students and you all as potential students

389
00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:37.980
coming into IED,

390
00:23:39.301 --> 00:23:42.630
and to sort of reassure you in a way that there's no standard definition of IED

391
00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:47.280
students. I think you'll find this largely across the RCA, but there might be other

392
00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:50.040
disciplines where you think, well, you're probably going to come in with a certain

393
00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:53.580
set of expertise. You've got a certain set of knowledge already, or you're coming

394
00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:58.920
from one of a few different undergraduate programs or coming from a certain set of

395
00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:00.810
careers or disciplines.

396
00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:05.611
But really on IED, we are very open to all people from all kinds of backgrounds,

397
00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:11.310
and it's really that wealth and that variety of backgrounds that really helps us be

398
00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:14.220
a really strong course and help each other to produce really interesting

399
00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:18.390
experiences that cross disciplines, that cross creative practice.

400
00:24:19.801 --> 00:24:23.280
So no matter where you're from, potentially there's a home for you in IED.

401
00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:27.301
I would say overall, IED students tend to be curious.

402
00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:30.871
So you're wanting to find things out, and the research is a big element to what

403
00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:35.250
we're doing, and testing things and trying them and seeing an idea and actually

404
00:24:35.611 --> 00:24:38.340
making it and trying it and testing it and thinking through making.

405
00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:42.690
That's all part of our ethos, and that goes with a sense of curiosity.

406
00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:47.310
They're often socially engaged. So as you'll see from the student work, a lot of

407
00:24:47.310 --> 00:24:50.700
them are dealing with particular issues, that the information they're trying to

408
00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:54.060
tell the world, the message they're trying to get across through these experiences

409
00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:59.551
are about important, urgent issues, things which are facing the whole of the world

410
00:24:59.611 --> 00:25:01.621
or things which are facing very specific communities.

411
00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:05.820
An issue that is actually at the heart of this particular community and how it

412
00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:08.820
affects them and how we need to change things in order to help them.

413
00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:12.090
And these are quite often things that come out of IED projects.

414
00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:17.340
IED students are collaborative. We want you working together as much as possible,

415
00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:21.030
and there'll be various points where we're really inviting you to do that.

416
00:25:21.301 --> 00:25:24.361
But really to produce the kind of work and the quality of work that we expect on

417
00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:26.130
IED, you're going to have to work with others.

418
00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:28.770
You're going to have to bring other people into your process and be generous with

419
00:25:28.830 --> 00:25:30.240
your time and your knowledge as well.

420
00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:33.030
So we'll be encouraging you to do that as much as possible.

421
00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:37.710
As I said earlier, experimental, trying things out, not willing to sort of take the

422
00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:40.351
normal path through, but thinking about different ways.

423
00:25:40.741 --> 00:25:45.930
Not using tools or methods in a way that maybe they would be expected to use, but

424
00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:48.840
thinking about, okay, well, how can I do this differently, and then testing that,

425
00:25:48.930 --> 00:25:51.870
trying it. Maybe it won't always work. There'll be failures.

426
00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:52.830
There'll be things that don't quite work.

427
00:25:52.861 --> 00:25:55.801
But all that will be a learning process and will lead you to more interesting

428
00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:57.030
experiences at the end.

429
00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:02.340
So no matter what everyone's background, no matter what your discipline, all IED

430
00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:05.910
students focus on experience. So that's really what we're producing.

431
00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:10.530
So there could be lots of different physical outcomes that you are making or

432
00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:12.571
digital virtual outcomes that you are making.

433
00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:15.780
Maybe you already have lots of skills and expertise in making.

434
00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:20.280
But we will be looking at it through the lens of experience in particular.

435
00:26:20.491 --> 00:26:22.680
So what does that mean as an experience?

436
00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:26.520
And even if you're producing something that's digital on a screen, even if you're

437
00:26:26.551 --> 00:26:30.720
making a piece of moving image, a film, we'll be thinking about what the experience

438
00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:33.540
is that surrounds that. What is the experience of watching this film?

439
00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:36.030
How are you presenting it to people? Who are those people?

440
00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:41.071
What is the time and space and location where this film is being shown, and how can

441
00:26:41.101 --> 00:26:44.491
we design for that in order to make it more impactful, to make it an even more

442
00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:45.660
interesting experience?

443
00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:49.801
So yeah, that's a key thing that we're looking for is people who are wanting to

444
00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:55.590
engage with experiences, multi-sensory, multimodal, across time, and specific to

445
00:26:55.620 --> 00:26:59.250
certain contexts. So all those things that happened in the last bullet points as

446
00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:03.720
well. So not just one type of outcome, but taking that look out and saying, "Okay,

447
00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:06.720
what is the experience that surrounds this as an outcome?" That's what we're

448
00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:09.361
looking for in IED designers and students.

449
00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:15.930
Okay. Just as a final thing, I'll say, if you want to know more about IED students,

450
00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:19.380
hopefully you've already seen this, but if you haven't already, you can look at the

451
00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:25.290
showcase for the year we just had. So the 2026 showcase link is on the screen

452
00:27:25.351 --> 00:27:27.720
now, but also there's a QR code you can go directly to it.

453
00:27:28.080 --> 00:27:29.910
And this will take you to the RCA showcase page.

454
00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:32.730
You can see all the programs there, but it should take you specifically to the one

455
00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:36.660
about IED. Have a look in those, look through the students' work, see what kind of

456
00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:39.930
things they're doing. You can also look at previous years as well.

457
00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:42.720
So you can Google that, or actually, I think if you just change where it says

458
00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:46.830
2026.RCA, you can change that to 2025, 2024.

459
00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:48.660
You can look back through previous years as well.

460
00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:51.720
And that's a good way, really, of seeing the range of stuff we do.

461
00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:54.991
And really, we're then looking to you to think, okay, what's it going to be next

462
00:27:55.051 --> 00:28:00.571
year? What's the next sort of set of experiences that IED is producing for both

463
00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:00.930
those?

464
00:28:02.592 --> 00:28:05.922
And then the second question on there: are students' directions or pathways within

465
00:28:06.013 --> 00:28:09.222
IED determined by the projects submitted in their application portfolios?

466
00:28:10.182 --> 00:28:13.872
Or can students choose and reshape their direction after joining the program?

467
00:28:13.932 --> 00:28:14.682
Another great question.

468
00:28:16.063 --> 00:28:20.802
It is open, so you do not have to submit an idea in your application which you then

469
00:28:20.832 --> 00:28:22.692
have to go through and have to spend a whole year doing that.

470
00:28:24.042 --> 00:28:25.692
I think that'd be really restrictive if you did that.

471
00:28:25.752 --> 00:28:29.442
We want this to be a transformative process for you, so we're expecting to come in

472
00:28:29.472 --> 00:28:34.632
and try different things, try different experiments, and learn new skills and new

473
00:28:34.662 --> 00:28:38.802
approaches. So, yeah, hopefully you've got the chance to do that.

474
00:28:39.432 --> 00:28:42.192
And therefore, we don't say, "Right, you've got to tell us on day one what your

475
00:28:42.252 --> 00:28:46.422
idea is, and then you stick with that idea." There's always a chance to adapt,

476
00:28:46.572 --> 00:28:48.912
develop, to take it in slightly new directions.

477
00:28:50.621 --> 00:28:55.152
The only thing I would say within that is you have a year to do this MA, and that

478
00:28:55.692 --> 00:28:58.332
we don't want you to spend too long at the beginning sort of thinking, "Well, I

479
00:28:58.362 --> 00:29:02.922
could do one of these dozen different ideas and I'm not quite sure what." We will

480
00:29:02.953 --> 00:29:05.832
be working with you, your tutors will be working with you to sort of settle on an

481
00:29:05.922 --> 00:29:10.212
idea fairly soon in the first term, or at least a sort of broad direction for your

482
00:29:10.272 --> 00:29:13.393
work. It's these kind of ideas, these kind of things I'm looking to do.

483
00:29:14.533 --> 00:29:19.302
So that would allow you to then adapt it. But yeah, we want to be on a trajectory.

484
00:29:19.332 --> 00:29:22.362
We want something that you're working with so you can start making work and start

485
00:29:22.422 --> 00:29:26.953
testing things. And it is helpful, therefore, in your application, if you give us a

486
00:29:27.013 --> 00:29:31.482
sense of that. So if we know you coming in have got kind of like, it's this area

487
00:29:31.513 --> 00:29:34.572
that I'm looking at, and there's a particular set of questions I'm interested in,

488
00:29:34.632 --> 00:29:38.682
or I've already done this work that sort of led, it seems to be leading in this

489
00:29:38.712 --> 00:29:40.182
direction, I want to continue that.

490
00:29:40.513 --> 00:29:43.572
So please do give us a sense of that, but you are not locked into that.

491
00:29:43.632 --> 00:29:47.203
If you then turn up and say, "Actually, I've discovered maybe through the first

492
00:29:47.263 --> 00:29:50.203
lectures and through the first workshops and stuff you're happening, that's

493
00:29:50.442 --> 00:29:53.442
something I really want to do that's different to that," you are able to do that.

494
00:29:54.132 --> 00:29:56.232
So you're not completely locked in, but do have a sense.

495
00:29:56.322 --> 00:29:59.652
Do kind of give us an idea of the direction that you're interested in going and the

496
00:29:59.742 --> 00:30:01.902
type of work that you want to do within IED.

497
00:30:04.362 --> 00:30:04.572
Okay.

498
00:30:05.832 --> 00:30:10.662
So the other questions coming in. So if applicants submit before the same round

499
00:30:10.692 --> 00:30:14.143
deadline, does the submitting earlier affect their chances of admission or the

500
00:30:14.203 --> 00:30:15.912
order in which portfolios are reviewed?

501
00:30:17.472 --> 00:30:18.222
No. So there

502
00:30:19.453 --> 00:30:22.842
is no priority given to different rounds.

503
00:30:24.042 --> 00:30:28.182
I think in general, across the RCA, we ask you to submit as early as possible.

504
00:30:28.632 --> 00:30:32.922
There might be cases, I guess, if a course was very oversubscribed and lots and

505
00:30:32.953 --> 00:30:33.402
lots of people

506
00:30:35.232 --> 00:30:38.472
put in an application in round one and were accepted, then maybe there was slightly

507
00:30:38.502 --> 00:30:40.752
less spaces available for the next rounds.

508
00:30:40.962 --> 00:30:43.842
So it is worth coming in earlier if you can.

509
00:30:44.563 --> 00:30:46.752
But it's not ranked by order in any way.

510
00:30:46.813 --> 00:30:49.122
We're not looking at those in the order that they're submitted.

511
00:30:50.143 --> 00:30:52.482
So yeah, you can submit at any round.

512
00:30:52.602 --> 00:30:56.232
I would recommend going for the earliest round that you can, so we're looking at

513
00:30:56.292 --> 00:31:00.042
work earlier on and we're able to make offers off the back of that sooner rather

514
00:31:00.072 --> 00:31:00.612
than later.

515
00:31:02.472 --> 00:31:04.092
Okay, I hope that answers that one.

516
00:31:06.643 --> 00:31:07.033
So,

517
00:31:08.862 --> 00:31:11.412
what do you look for in a student applying for IED?

518
00:31:12.522 --> 00:31:16.302
So I guess going back to that last screen, really, in terms of the type of student,

519
00:31:16.332 --> 00:31:17.862
there isn't a set type of student,

520
00:31:19.063 --> 00:31:22.063
but there are various things which are helpful to look at.

521
00:31:22.602 --> 00:31:27.732
So I would say we're looking for people interested in experience in particular, and

522
00:31:27.763 --> 00:31:31.212
that's maybe also where there's a slight difference with other programs.

523
00:31:31.242 --> 00:31:34.422
For other programs, you might be able to apply for more than one of the programs.

524
00:31:34.482 --> 00:31:36.252
Maybe we cross over in certain areas.

525
00:31:37.332 --> 00:31:42.252
But I think, think about the experiences that you're looking to make on the course.

526
00:31:42.612 --> 00:31:46.962
When we're looking at portfolios, in particular, it's nice to see people working

527
00:31:47.022 --> 00:31:49.242
across different modes of experience.

528
00:31:49.813 --> 00:31:55.182
So in other words, if we do see portfolios which are screen design, here's

529
00:31:55.992 --> 00:31:59.862
three different apps that I made, and here's the screen designs for each one of

530
00:31:59.893 --> 00:32:02.862
those, that doesn't really give us a sense of the experience.

531
00:32:02.893 --> 00:32:05.982
It doesn't necessarily give us a sense that actually you've thought about multiple

532
00:32:06.042 --> 00:32:08.352
senses, you've thought about the people who are interacting with this.

533
00:32:08.742 --> 00:32:09.852
So you might present the same thing.

534
00:32:09.882 --> 00:32:12.732
It might still be app designs, but here's it being used in real life.

535
00:32:12.792 --> 00:32:15.703
Here's actually how it ties in with this other form of experience, or here's this

536
00:32:16.783 --> 00:32:21.672
facilitated session that goes with this app, or a workshop that leads into it, or

537
00:32:21.732 --> 00:32:24.222
an experience in a commercial space or whatever.

538
00:32:24.763 --> 00:32:26.772
So thinking about experience, I think is important.

539
00:32:27.222 --> 00:32:29.172
Thinking about where you want to go.

540
00:32:29.292 --> 00:32:34.272
It's really helpful when we're looking at portfolios is to know that what do you

541
00:32:34.302 --> 00:32:37.542
want to get out of IED? What are the things you want to learn? How can we help you?

542
00:32:37.632 --> 00:32:40.002
Can we help you get to where you want to get to?

543
00:32:40.332 --> 00:32:42.042
So we're looking at things in those terms as well.

544
00:32:42.342 --> 00:32:45.552
And then apart from that, the things I said in the slide, so curiosity,

545
00:32:45.852 --> 00:32:48.432
experimentation, collaboration is key.

546
00:32:48.522 --> 00:32:51.612
So we want to see that you can and want to work with other people as well.

547
00:32:52.033 --> 00:32:53.502
Not all work needs to be collaborative.

548
00:32:53.563 --> 00:32:57.132
In your independent research project, it is independent. You do that yourself.

549
00:32:57.703 --> 00:33:01.242
But just in the nature of the type of work we do, it's really difficult to do all

550
00:33:01.272 --> 00:33:04.362
those things yourself. If you want to create a multidisciplinary, multi-sensory

551
00:33:04.422 --> 00:33:07.482
experience, then probably going to need some other people's help in doing that to a

552
00:33:07.513 --> 00:33:08.263
really good level.

553
00:33:10.422 --> 00:33:13.362
So yeah, I hope that helps. But yeah, do look back at those slides if you need to

554
00:33:13.422 --> 00:33:13.842
as well.

555
00:33:15.672 --> 00:33:20.082
So the current website asks for one PDF covering up to two projects for 2027 entry.

556
00:33:20.263 --> 00:33:25.332
Is there a page limit, file size limits, or recommended number of pages per

557
00:33:25.662 --> 00:33:26.112
project?

558
00:33:27.732 --> 00:33:31.182
I think at this stage, we're going with what you read there as the requirements.

559
00:33:33.102 --> 00:33:36.372
We might review that and just see the amount of projects within that.

560
00:33:36.432 --> 00:33:39.942
And sometimes there are differences within that from year to year.

561
00:33:40.272 --> 00:33:42.222
I'm not sure what the file size limit is.

562
00:33:42.283 --> 00:33:44.982
I think you'd need to see that within the system itself. There is a limit.

563
00:33:45.072 --> 00:33:48.252
I think if you try to upload something that's absolutely massive, then it might

564
00:33:48.283 --> 00:33:52.302
have issues with that. I would say the important things in terms of what you're

565
00:33:52.332 --> 00:33:57.192
including in the portfolio, in your projects, and the amount of space you're giving

566
00:33:57.192 --> 00:34:01.302
to that, the size of it, the amount of projects, is just be mindful of us reviewing

567
00:34:01.362 --> 00:34:05.592
it. What we want to do is we want to see your work and see you.

568
00:34:05.802 --> 00:34:09.522
See there's a student journey part where you're talking about yourself with text.

569
00:34:09.822 --> 00:34:13.243
There's a video which is really important, where you get to talk to us.

570
00:34:13.993 --> 00:34:18.283
So just a video talking about your journey, what you're aiming to achieve, what

571
00:34:18.312 --> 00:34:22.902
your interests are. But then the portfolio is something we want to look at, and I

572
00:34:22.962 --> 00:34:24.942
guess we want to see some variety straight away.

573
00:34:24.972 --> 00:34:28.513
It'd be useful to see that there's different types of work you've got in there.

574
00:34:29.442 --> 00:34:32.172
You've thought about experience, you've thought about testing things.

575
00:34:32.231 --> 00:34:35.592
You told us how you work, maybe the things that work really well, maybe things that

576
00:34:35.623 --> 00:34:38.143
don't or you've learned from, that's useful to see as well.

577
00:34:39.672 --> 00:34:41.772
And I would say brevity is important.

578
00:34:41.832 --> 00:34:46.752
So sometimes we get portfolios which are 35 pages long, and regardless of what the

579
00:34:46.873 --> 00:34:52.002
size of it or the megabyte limit is, people pack as much as possible in there, and

580
00:34:52.033 --> 00:34:55.572
you really have to zoom into this PDF to see loads and loads of text.

581
00:34:56.292 --> 00:34:57.762
That's really hard for us to go through.

582
00:34:57.822 --> 00:35:00.912
It takes a long time to look at that, and therefore, we probably don't have a lot

583
00:35:00.942 --> 00:35:04.123
of time then to actually really think about the work and what it's looking at.

584
00:35:04.452 --> 00:35:07.602
We're just looking through lots and lots of stuff, and we probably run out of time

585
00:35:07.632 --> 00:35:10.662
to do that. So try and keep it short and sweet.

586
00:35:10.752 --> 00:35:11.772
Do focus in on

587
00:35:13.332 --> 00:35:17.352
a couple of really good projects that maybe show what your kind of work is, what

588
00:35:17.382 --> 00:35:18.373
you're interested in.

589
00:35:20.052 --> 00:35:23.232
Don't write loads and loads and loads of text because it just won't be possible to

590
00:35:23.262 --> 00:35:26.592
read it all. Don't cram loads of stuff in there. Show us images.

591
00:35:26.862 --> 00:35:30.132
Show us the outcomes of your work. Show us pictures of you working.

592
00:35:30.222 --> 00:35:33.373
Show us pictures of people engaging with your work or you collaborating with

593
00:35:33.432 --> 00:35:35.172
others. That's always helpful to see.

594
00:35:35.412 --> 00:35:38.382
And give us enough text that we know we can see what the project's about and we can

595
00:35:38.412 --> 00:35:43.512
understand, get the context of it. But beyond that, look at the admissions site.

596
00:35:43.542 --> 00:35:44.862
It will have more information on there.

597
00:35:45.552 --> 00:35:48.462
And if there's any reviews of that, and if we add any more page limits or anything,

598
00:35:48.643 --> 00:35:50.442
that will be on there when you come to apply as well.

599
00:35:52.902 --> 00:35:57.042
Okay. What current projects with companies, cultural organizations, or exhibitions

600
00:35:57.072 --> 00:35:58.842
can IED students actually take part in?

601
00:35:59.442 --> 00:36:02.772
Can you share a few specific examples of recent graduates, including companies,

602
00:36:02.862 --> 00:36:06.672
studios, exhibitions they joined, and the roles and creative practices they

603
00:36:06.702 --> 00:36:09.252
pursued? Yeah, there's a line there.

604
00:36:09.522 --> 00:36:14.832
In terms of link-ups with other organizations, I can tell you ones that we've had

605
00:36:14.862 --> 00:36:15.282
recently.

606
00:36:16.632 --> 00:36:21.462
So for example, the most recent one we've had just last week, we have an annual

607
00:36:21.492 --> 00:36:26.382
exhibition at the V&amp;A in London. So we take part in a digital design weekend,

608
00:36:27.552 --> 00:36:32.742
where IED student work from the previous year, so from graduating students, is

609
00:36:32.802 --> 00:36:38.262
shown in the V&amp;A as part of that event. So some work is selected.

610
00:36:38.322 --> 00:36:41.772
The V&amp;A curators come in, choose certain bits of work, and it's put into that

611
00:36:41.832 --> 00:36:45.822
context. And that work takes in design work as well as sort of artistic work.

612
00:36:46.212 --> 00:36:49.722
So some of it's very applied, commercial, some of it's more experimental.

613
00:36:50.862 --> 00:36:52.482
But all that is shown in the V&amp;A.

614
00:36:53.718 --> 00:36:57.258
We have various other link-ups with galleries and spaces.

615
00:36:57.288 --> 00:37:02.508
So we have spaces in London where work will be shown, and that'll be led by

616
00:37:02.568 --> 00:37:03.978
individual field collective leads.

617
00:37:04.038 --> 00:37:07.248
So usually tutors will organize something around the space that they have a

618
00:37:07.279 --> 00:37:08.088
relationship with.

619
00:37:09.648 --> 00:37:15.529
In terms of other collaboration partners, so we have a regular link-up with Meta,

620
00:37:16.728 --> 00:37:20.118
and looking at their mixed reality groups.

621
00:37:20.148 --> 00:37:23.208
So thinking about AR and VR and the technology they have for that.

622
00:37:23.658 --> 00:37:28.398
So we have run, in the past, workshops where our IED students can apply to take

623
00:37:28.428 --> 00:37:33.288
part in a workshop, work on a brief with Meta, and that they will then give

624
00:37:33.318 --> 00:37:36.438
feedback on the work, and you'll get a chance to use their technologies and their

625
00:37:36.468 --> 00:37:39.438
approaches. So they will teach into that as well.

626
00:37:41.088 --> 00:37:44.148
We have various other projects. We have climate companies.

627
00:37:44.208 --> 00:37:48.258
We've just done a project with a company called Outseeds, which look at climate

628
00:37:48.288 --> 00:37:51.048
data. And they funded a

629
00:37:53.538 --> 00:37:58.578
placement for students from last year. So they took part in a design sprint.

630
00:37:58.788 --> 00:38:04.699
They made proposals for how to basically share climate data

631
00:38:04.728 --> 00:38:08.748
with children in the context of classrooms, actually portable classrooms that they

632
00:38:08.808 --> 00:38:14.568
take into schools. And that company also funded, yeah, not only that design sprint,

633
00:38:14.658 --> 00:38:19.038
but then the people who were successful in that design sprints had a placement with

634
00:38:19.098 --> 00:38:20.779
them where they got to take that work further.

635
00:38:22.158 --> 00:38:25.878
There's lots of things like that that happen, and it does adapt and change each

636
00:38:25.908 --> 00:38:27.888
year. So there's more things that keep coming in.

637
00:38:27.918 --> 00:38:31.727
And as the students change, we adapt to that and your interests.

638
00:38:32.268 --> 00:38:34.008
So there's lots of things that happen at that level.

639
00:38:34.038 --> 00:38:36.138
There's lots of things that happen at RCA level as well.

640
00:38:37.248 --> 00:38:40.128
In terms of directions that students go into, again, it's really varied.

641
00:38:41.208 --> 00:38:43.548
We have lots of people maybe go into

642
00:38:44.748 --> 00:38:49.638
agency-type practice. That's either working for design agencies, interaction

643
00:38:49.699 --> 00:38:53.718
experience design agencies, immersive design agencies,

644
00:38:55.128 --> 00:38:57.348
usually around London, but lots of other places as well.

645
00:38:57.408 --> 00:39:01.038
We have lots of students who set up their own companies as well, so their own

646
00:39:01.098 --> 00:39:04.038
agency or all types of companies to do that kind of work, usually with

647
00:39:04.068 --> 00:39:07.518
collaboration with other people from the RCA or from an IED as well.

648
00:39:08.898 --> 00:39:11.118
We have people who go into fine art practice.

649
00:39:12.798 --> 00:39:15.529
And we support lots of students even after they've graduated.

650
00:39:15.618 --> 00:39:21.438
We help them with funding proposals, with proposals to galleries, museum

651
00:39:21.529 --> 00:39:24.498
spaces. We have people who then go and work in those spaces.

652
00:39:24.529 --> 00:39:30.438
So we have curators, curation people come out of IED and then go on to

653
00:39:30.529 --> 00:39:32.958
help design experiences for galleries and museums.

654
00:39:34.638 --> 00:39:39.108
Yeah, there's probably too many to list in total, but hopefully, that gives you an

655
00:39:39.199 --> 00:39:39.978
overall idea.

656
00:39:42.618 --> 00:39:45.558
Okay, there's a few more. Let's see if we can get through these.

657
00:39:46.728 --> 00:39:49.608
Is a personal statement required in the application, and what's the best way to

658
00:39:49.638 --> 00:39:54.018
approach writing it? Also, what is the expectation of how a submitted portfolio

659
00:39:54.048 --> 00:39:57.618
should be structured? So we touched on some of this already.

660
00:39:58.608 --> 00:40:02.058
Yes, a personal statement is required, so you need to write out a statement.

661
00:40:03.468 --> 00:40:07.157
Best way to approach writing it is, honestly,

662
00:40:07.247 --> 00:40:13.158
inevitably, I think we see a lot of writing now which is maybe being supported by

663
00:40:13.248 --> 00:40:18.529
AI, which at one level is okay. We all use AI perhaps to support writing and to

664
00:40:18.558 --> 00:40:20.178
word things differently.

665
00:40:21.558 --> 00:40:23.718
I think if you're generating the whole thing through AI, that's usually quite

666
00:40:23.808 --> 00:40:26.478
obvious that actually we haven't really learned much about you as a person.

667
00:40:27.138 --> 00:40:30.678
So try and get yourself across. This is your personal statement.

668
00:40:30.768 --> 00:40:35.388
We can see your work, but we want to know about you, and we want to know a bit

669
00:40:35.418 --> 00:40:38.508
about your background, where you're coming from and what your journey's been up to

670
00:40:38.538 --> 00:40:41.658
this point. We want to know about where you want your journey to go.

671
00:40:42.318 --> 00:40:47.238
So what is it that you want to achieve, and how is IED going to help you to do

672
00:40:47.268 --> 00:40:50.418
that? What do you want to achieve through this course, and where's that leading to?

673
00:40:50.478 --> 00:40:52.098
Is there a certain type of job you're looking for?

674
00:40:52.158 --> 00:40:54.798
Is there a certain type of work you're looking to do at the end of that?

675
00:40:56.178 --> 00:40:58.848
And I think really importantly as well, why IED?

676
00:40:59.449 --> 00:41:01.848
There are lots of programs at the RCA, as we've said.

677
00:41:01.878 --> 00:41:05.058
There are ones that have slight crossovers with what IED does, but there is a

678
00:41:05.118 --> 00:41:10.248
particular approach in IED, again, about experience, about communication, and about

679
00:41:10.279 --> 00:41:13.038
a way of working perhaps, which you won't get in other programs.

680
00:41:13.098 --> 00:41:18.199
We want you to think about that. Why is this the best place for you as opposed to

681
00:41:18.228 --> 00:41:20.808
other programs, other programs in the UK or abroad?

682
00:41:21.378 --> 00:41:23.718
What is it about IED in particular that interests you?

683
00:41:24.768 --> 00:41:26.328
You don't need to tell us about why IED.

684
00:41:26.598 --> 00:41:31.248
Sometimes people fall into that trap a bit as well, where it's like, "I really want

685
00:41:31.248 --> 00:41:34.488
to be on IED because of you do this, and you do this, and you do this, and you do

686
00:41:34.518 --> 00:41:36.048
this." It's like, okay, we know we do that.

687
00:41:36.108 --> 00:41:40.158
So use your space to actually talk about you and what you're interested in doing as

688
00:41:40.188 --> 00:41:40.548
well.

689
00:41:42.588 --> 00:41:46.068
Is there an expectation of how a submitted portfolio should be best structured?

690
00:41:47.898 --> 00:41:50.178
One thing, we will have follow-up sessions on portfolios.

691
00:41:50.238 --> 00:41:52.758
So I will be doing more of these where actually I'll go through some examples of

692
00:41:52.788 --> 00:41:55.308
portfolios themselves or structures for portfolios.

693
00:41:56.388 --> 00:41:59.478
I think the only thing I would say at this stage is, in addition to what I said

694
00:41:59.508 --> 00:42:04.458
earlier about not trying to cram too much in, we see portfolios come through where

695
00:42:04.878 --> 00:42:10.279
it's PDFs, landscape pages, very wide, lots of information packed in there, looking

696
00:42:10.338 --> 00:42:14.088
at different projects, and lots and lots of different pages.

697
00:42:14.538 --> 00:42:18.798
And they take five minutes to download and view because they're all 500 megabytes

698
00:42:18.828 --> 00:42:23.358
or something. And it's so dense that it's really hard to scroll through this whole

699
00:42:23.449 --> 00:42:25.398
thing and then go to the next page and scroll through the whole thing.

700
00:42:25.938 --> 00:42:27.258
So I would try and do it

701
00:42:28.638 --> 00:42:33.558
in a way that is most clear for us viewing it. We can open up a PDF.

702
00:42:33.888 --> 00:42:36.529
We can see all the pages, easily downloadable.

703
00:42:36.588 --> 00:42:38.988
It's not crammed full of images, not crammed full of text.

704
00:42:40.008 --> 00:42:44.688
But we can see key images, hero images of what your work is, how you're working,

705
00:42:45.018 --> 00:42:47.388
and a bit of text alongside that really to give us some context.

706
00:42:47.688 --> 00:42:50.898
Maybe tell us about your different approaches, how you research, how you make

707
00:42:50.928 --> 00:42:56.328
things, that kind of information in there. But avoid denseness.

708
00:42:56.508 --> 00:43:01.938
Avoid really massive, wide, huge PDFs that are hundreds and hundreds of megabytes

709
00:43:01.998 --> 00:43:05.029
and contain way too much information we could possibly look at.

710
00:43:06.378 --> 00:43:10.128
It's about communication. You want to be communication designers, information

711
00:43:10.158 --> 00:43:14.328
designers. So think about how that information is structured, make it human, make

712
00:43:14.388 --> 00:43:17.268
it possible for us to look at, and don't put too much in there.

713
00:43:19.098 --> 00:43:19.279
Yeah.

714
00:43:22.067 --> 00:43:25.279
Okay, I think we're there. Thank you very much for your time.

715
00:43:25.338 --> 00:43:27.258
Thank you for your interest in IED.

716
00:43:27.768 --> 00:43:30.498
I'm just going to put in my email address as well.

717
00:43:31.158 --> 00:43:35.358
So if there is anything off the back of this that you're interested in finding out

718
00:43:35.418 --> 00:43:39.978
about a bit more, or particular questions you've got, then you can email me on

719
00:43:40.038 --> 00:43:42.918
that. If not, I will be doing more of these sessions.

720
00:43:42.949 --> 00:43:44.928
We'll have more stuff involving these sessions coming up as well.

721
00:43:45.408 --> 00:43:50.058
So keep an eye out for them on the RCA website where you found about this one, and

722
00:43:50.118 --> 00:43:53.058
join the sessions where we can go into more detail and we can ask more questions.

723
00:43:54.618 --> 00:43:58.188
Okay. Thank you very much, everyone. Great to see you all. Bye.
